+# Hegel property-based testing report — dyon
+
+Property-based tests were added to `tests/lib.rs` (module `hegel_tests`) using
+`hegeltest` (crate `hegel`). They exercise dyon's public module/runtime API
+(`load_str`, `run_str`, `Module`, `Runtime`, `Call`) on generated source. dyon
+represents all numbers as `f64`, so numeric behavior is checked against a Rust
+`f64` oracle.
+
+## 1. What was tested (properties)
+
+Ten live properties plus a pinned known-failure and three ignored hang
+reproducers.
+
+| # | Test | Property |
+|---|------|----------|
+| 1 | `prop_arithmetic_matches_rust_oracle` | A fully-parenthesized `+ - * / %` expression tree over exactly-representable integer leaves evaluates bit-identically (NaN-aware) in dyon and in a Rust `f64` oracle. Parens fix evaluation order, so this isolates the operations. |
+| 2 | `prop_operator_precedence_matches_oracle` | An **un-parenthesized** operator chain is evaluated by dyon with `* / %` binding tighter than `+ -`, both left-associative — checked against a transcribed-precedence oracle (two left-to-right folds). |
+| 3 | `prop_parenthesization_is_neutral` | Wrapping a `+`/`-` chain in redundant parentheses does not change the result (dyon-vs-dyon). |
+| 4 | `prop_string_concat_matches_rust` | dyon `a + b` on strings equals Rust string concatenation (full-Unicode `text()`). |
+| 5 | `prop_boolean_logic_matches_rust` | A fully-parenthesized boolean expression (`&&`, `||`, `!`) over `true`/`false` matches a Rust bool oracle. |
+| 6 | `prop_run_is_deterministic` | Evaluating the same source twice (fresh `Runtime` each time) yields the same result (`Ok`/`Err` and value). |
+| 7 | `prop_array_indexing_and_bounds` | In-range integer indices return the stored element; indices `>= len` return `Err` (never a panic). |
+| 8 | `prop_load_never_panics_on_arbitrary_text` | `load_str` on arbitrary `text()` returns `Ok`/`Err`, never panics. |
+| 9 | `prop_load_never_panics_on_token_soup` | `load_str` on random sequences of real dyon tokens/keywords never panics. |
+| 10 | `prop_shallow_nesting_no_abort` | Sweeping nesting depth (parens, blocks, `if`, unary `-`, `+` chains) in the **safe** depth zone, `load_str` returns without panic/abort. |
+
+Highest-value per the brief: **#1 arithmetic-vs-oracle** and **#10 /
+nesting** (the hang reproducers below).
+
+## 2. Bugs found
+
+### BUG A — decimal literal parsing is not correctly rounded (pinned, failing)
+
+`prop_float_literal_parse_correctly_rounded_KNOWN_FAILURE` (deterministic,
+always fails).
+
+dyon's decimal→`f64` parser does not return the nearest `f64` to the literal:
+
+| literal | correct f64 (Rust) bits | dyon bits | note |
+|---------|------------------------|-----------|------|
+| `1.4375` | `0x3ff7000000000000` | `0x3ff6ffffffffffff` | **exactly representable**, dyon returns the value one ULP below |
+| `0.3` | `0x3fd3333333333333` | `0x3fd3333333333334` | one ULP high |
+| `3.14159` | `0x400921f9f01b866e` | `0x400921f9f01b866f` | one ULP high |
+
+`1.4375 = 23/16` is exactly representable in binary floating point, so this is
+not an under-specified rounding corner — a numeric literal in a dyon program
+does not denote the value it appears to. Rust's `f64` parser (the oracle) is
+guaranteed correctly-rounded. This is oracle-independent in spirit (the exact
+case has a single correct answer). Root cause is dyon's number tokenizer
+(`read_token`-based number reading), not the arithmetic evaluator.
+
+This bug is the reason properties #1, #2 and #7 use **integer-valued leaves**:
+integers within 2^53 are exact and parse exactly, isolating the arithmetic
+operations under test from the literal-parsing defect (which has its own
+dedicated test).
+
+### BUG B — exponential-time parser blowup on deep nesting (hang / DoS)
+
+Three `#[ignore]`d reproducers: `nested_parens_parser_hang`,
+`nested_blocks_parser_hang`, `nested_if_parser_hang`.
+
+The parser takes time exponential in nesting depth for several recursive
+constructs, so a small, untrusted script can hang a host that calls
+`load`/`run` (denial of service). Measured for nested parens `f() = (((…1…)))`:
+
+| depth | parse time |
+|-------|-----------|
+| 5 | ~1 s |
+| 6 | ~9 s |
+| 7 | did not finish in 60 s |
+
+Nested blocks (`{ { … } }`) and nested `if/else` hang similarly (depth ~50 did
+not finish; depth 10 was instant). This is a **hang**, not a stack overflow or
+panic: the process spins on CPU and never returns (no per-test timeout would
+catch it, and an un-ignored test would take the whole suite down — hence
+`#[ignore]`). The reproducers use depth 40/80 to guarantee the hang if invoked
+explicitly:
+
+```
+cargo test --test lib -- --ignored nested_parens_parser_hang
+```
+
+## 3. Design notes / oracle choices
+
+- **Arithmetic oracle**: expression trees are built in Rust, rendered to dyon
+  source AND evaluated in Rust `f64` simultaneously, then compared NaN-aware.
+  dyon's `+ - * / %` map directly to Rust `f64` ops (`*n += b`, `%= b`, etc. in
+  `src/runtime/mod.rs`), so results are bit-identical when leaves parse
+  exactly. Division/modulo by zero matches Rust (`1/0 = inf`, `-1/0 = -inf`,
+  `0/0 = NaN`) — no error, per IEEE.
+- **Precedence oracle**: transcribed from the grammar (`assets/syntax.txt`):
+  `mul` (`* / %`) binds tighter than `add` (`+ -`), both left-associative;
+  `^`/pow is not chainable without parens. Leaves kept non-negative to isolate
+  precedence from unary-minus parsing.
+- **Depth bounds** on the arithmetic/nesting generators are documented in-code
+  as protecting the *test's runtime* against BUG B, not as hiding a contract
+  violation. The `prop_shallow_nesting_no_abort` property deliberately stays in
+  the safe zone; BUG B lives in the ignored reproducers.
+- Leaves in the differential numeric properties are **integers** to route
+  around BUG A (see above).
+
+## 4. Quirks observed (not pinned as bugs)
+
+- **Array index coercion**: a negative index (`a[-1]`) and `NaN` index both
+  return element 0; a fractional index truncates (`a[1.5]` → element 1);
+  `a[inf]` returns `Out of bounds`. Silent clamping of negative/NaN indices to
+  0 is surprising but not obviously a contract violation, so property #7
+  restricts to non-negative integer indices and only asserts the clean
+  in-range / out-of-range-`Err` contract.
+- Array element access requires `clone(a[i])` inside a function returning
+  `f64`; a bare `return a[i]` fails lifetime checking (`a does not live long
+  enough`) — expected dyon semantics.
+
+## 5. How to run
+
+```
+cargo test --test lib hegel_tests           # 10 live pass, 1 KNOWN_FAILURE fails, 3 ignored
+cargo test --test lib -- --ignored          # runs the hang reproducers (will hang — see BUG B)
+```
+
+- 10 live properties pass; `prop_float_literal_parse_correctly_rounded_KNOWN_FAILURE`
+  fails deterministically (BUG A); 3 reproducers are `#[ignore]`d (BUG B).
+- Liveness was verified by inverting property #1's assertion (hegel reported a
+  shrunk counterexample); reverted.
+- Exploratory high-count runs (400 cases) of the differential properties
+  surfaced no additional failures beyond BUG A.
+- `.hegel/` added to `.gitignore`.
